\section*{Critical center magnetization in fixed-boundary XY boxes}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. For aligned square-boundary XY spins, the proposed center magnetization is $A n^{-1/8}(\log n)^{1/16}(1+o(1))$, conditional on the listed companion inputs.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} For angles $\theta_x\in\mathbb R/(2\pi\mathbb Z)$ on $\Lambda_R=[-R,R]^2\cap\mathbb Z^2$, use the free-boundary cosine law proportional to $\exp[b\sum_{\{x,y\}\subset\Lambda_R,\,|x-y|=1}\cos(\theta_x-\theta_y)]$, relative to product uniform angles. Count each edge once and omit exterior edges. Define
\[
 C_b(r)=\lim_{R\to\infty}\mathbb E_{R,b}^{\mathrm{free}}\cos(\theta_0-\theta_{re_1}),\quad
 m(b)=\lim_{r\to\infty}-r^{-1}\log C_b(r),\quad b_c=\inf\{b>0:m(b)=0\}.
\]
The thermodynamic limit precedes the separation limit. In $\Lambda_n$, now fix every boundary angle to zero and retain all internal bonds, including those incident to the boundary. Put $a_n=\mathbb E_{n,b_c}^{0}\cos\theta_0$. Using the companion height and spin-field constructions at critical height coefficient $a(b_c)=8\pi$, together with their finite Gaussian comparison, separated-pin restoration and local partition-map results, the theorem supplies $A_{\mathrm{XY}}>0$ with
\[
 a_n=A_{\mathrm{XY}}n^{-1/8}(\log n)^{1/16}(1+o(1)).
\]
The normalization is the actual centre magnetization, with inverse temperature fixed at the mass-defined $b_c$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Critical height, local renormalization, and spin-field estimates compare the boundary-pinned observable across scales. The Green-function normalization separates the universal power and marginal logarithm from the positive square-domain amplitude.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
For aligned square-boundary XY spins, the proposed center magnetization is $A n^{-1/8}(\log n)^{1/16}(1+o(1))$, conditional on the listed companion inputs. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

